# Day 13 calendar UI DTO integration review

## Scope and requirements

- Target: Day 13 local demo review; annual synthetic attendance fixture and shift calendar / payroll demonstration.
- RTM: R-15 isolated UTF-8 demo data; R-16 calendar event DTO and browser interaction; R-17 payroll UI/API and Excel export; R-18 independent audit and evidence status.
- Targeted source: `src/main/resources/templates/shift/calendar.html`; API contract: `src/main/java/com/example/kintai/domain/shift/dto/CalendarEventDto.java` and `ShiftApiController.java`; seed runner: `scripts/prepare_local_demo_db.ps1` and `scripts/local_demo_attendance.sql`; CSRF fetch handling: `src/main/resources/templates/common/layout.html`.

## Finding and change

The calendar template treated each `/api/shifts/events` response as a raw `ShiftDto` (`shiftId`, `workDate`, `startTime`, `endTime`, `status`). The controller actually returns `CalendarEventDto` (`id`, `title`, ISO `start`/`end`, `color`, and `extendedProps` containing `shiftId`, `staffName`, `status`, `plannedBreakMinutes`, and authorization-filtered memos). The mismatch threw while mapping events, so a populated API appeared as an empty calendar.

The template now preserves the API event fields, derives colors from `extendedProps.status`, and reads date/time from FullCalendar's event. The detail/confirmation modal reads `plannedBreakMinutes` and the start/end values from the calendar event. The demo database loader now feeds UTF-8 SQL files to the MySQL client as stdin, avoiding PowerShell pipeline code-page corruption; it also verifies shift counts and sample-name bytes.

The shared fetch interceptor prefers the raw `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie value with `X-XSRF-TOKEN` for mutation requests and falls back to the meta token when the cookie is absent. Both `CsrfCookieHeaderLifecycleIntegrationTest` and `CsrfSecurityIntegrationTest` are in the final regression JUnit output.

